WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.149 align:start position:0%
 
America's urban decline first began  during the 1950s the process of  suburbanization abetted by prosperity  massive automobile ownership and the  highway system plowed along during this  era as did the deindustrialization of  older cities during the 1920s industries  had left cities for suburbs like  Detroit's River Rouge but after World  War two industry was more likely to  leave the north altogether the  taft-hartley Act of 1948 enabled some  states to pass right-to-work laws which  made unionization far more difficult in  many southern states passed these laws  the work of tom holmes compares  manufacturing growth after 1947 in  counties right on the border of two  states one of which has a right-to-work  law the other of which is pro-union  Holmes found that manufacturing grew far  more in the pro-business states in the  pro-union states and almost all of  America's older colder cities were in  those pro-union states so the 1950s were  a tough time for many of those cities  Boston lost a hundred thousand people  during the decade a 13.7 percentage drop  in its population Detroit's population  shrank by a hundred and eighty thousand  or nine point seven percent Pittsburgh  also lost more than a tenth of its  population but during the 1950's the  population of New York City fell by only  one point four percent 120,000 people  New York was already proving to be  remarkably resilient over the longer  haul of the ten most populous US cities  in 1950 eight of the ten are at least 20  percent less populous today the two  exceptions are Los Angeles and New York  both of which have grown New York's  future looked enormous ly bright in the  early 1960s this was the era in which  glistening glass skyscrapers like Gordon  bunch as lever house and Mies van der  Rohe secret building were built along  Park Avenue the United Nations was  growing in Turtle Bay giving New Yorkers  the feeling that they were citizens of a  capital of the world  Broadway's musicals the works of Rodgers  and Hammerstein still helped define  American culture and Ed Sullivan reached  out from Broadway into tens of millions  of American homes every Sunday at 8:00  the Yankees dominated baseball and in  1961 Roger Maris finally bested Babe  Ruth's homerun record it was the age of  madmen and New York was on top  world 16 years later New York was  essentially bankrupt the city's  population fell from 7.8 million in 1960  to 7.1 million in 1980 by the deadly  burning summer of 1977 it seemed as if  all that bright future was gone what  went wrong how did such bright urban  promise dim so quickly we should start  with the economics the largest  industrial cluster in America in the  1950s was not automobile production in  Detroit  it was garment production in New York as  transportation cost plummeted it became  much cheaper to so ordinary garments in  low-wage southern states than in  Manhattan it was easy to ship them in  later decades it would be even cheaper  to make them in China and Bangladesh in  a few short years during the 1960's the  garment industry which had been a  mainstay of New York City since the  1820s shed hundreds of thousands of jobs  this same story repeated itself  throughout New York's once mighty  manufacturing base New York City's  harbor still thriving when Elliott Kazan  and Marlon Brando made on the waterfront  in 1954 was now made obsolete by New  Jersey's new Elizabeth Marine Terminal  the parent company of Domino Sugar  Refining once the city's most important  industry decided to expand its  facilities in Texas rather than its  landmark Brooklyn Factory in later years  other industries most notably finance  would make up for the lost manufacturing  employment but between 1969 and 1976 New  York lost about 600,000 jobs  social distress accompanied economic  dislocation for centuries New York City  has attracted the world's poor the  world's huddled masses yearning to be  free as Emma Lazarus put it Ellis Island  was the port of entry for millions of  immigrants to America and New York was  also the port of entry for hundreds of  thousands of African Americans fleeing  the Jim Crow South between 1940 and 1970  the number of african-americans in New  York City rose from four hundred sixty  thousand to 1.7 million New York has  benefited enormously from this flow of  talent the genius of the Harlem  Renaissance lit up the city in the world  Queens was blessed with the presence of  Louis Armstrong who was born in New  Orleans for almost 30 years yet the lay  you see a slavery meant that many  african-americans were poor like past  immigrants from Ireland in Eastern  Europe by the 1960s cities weren't  willing to just accept urban poverty  urban reformers like Jerome Cavanaugh  and Michigan and John Lindsay in New  York tried to use city resources to make  poverty less awful a completely laudable  aim but one that's difficult sometimes  to square with the limitations on what  cities can do poverty also came into the  spotlight because of New York City's  rising crime rates in 1957 there were  304 murders by 1961 that number had  increased to 483 in 1967 the year of the  Summer of Love and far less notably my  birth on the island of Manhattan the  number of New York homicides reached 746  by the time I was 5 in 1972 the number  of homicides doubled again to 1691 the  number of annual homicides would remain  over 1,500 for the rest of the decade it  hit 1814 in 1980 almost six times the  figure in 1957 there were safe parts of  the city when I was a kid but they had  shrunk to small amounts of walkable  space and children who grew up in  poverty also grew up looking over their  shoulders fearing robbery or worse Mayor  Lindsay's fun city had become a city of  fear abandoned spaces like the South  Bronx the spoke urban decay the graffiti  that seemed to be everywhere radiated  both urban chaos but also the creativity  that's still surged in the heart of the  city Lindsay may not have been able to  stop the crime wave but he does deserve  credit for helping to keep New York  essentially riot free in the tense hours  after Martin Luther King's death when  New York like much of urban America  seemed on the edge of explosion Lindsay  walked the streets of Harlem he was a  calming caring presence reminding  African American New Yorkers that he was  on their side and they largely believed  him Lindsay had flaws but his commitment  to racial justice could not be  questioned why did crime explode in New  York from 1957 to 1972 crime is  disproportionately an activity of young  men and the baby boom had given America  and New York a lot of young men by the  1960s  but changing demographics can only  explain a small amount maybe a fifth of  the rise in crime found in New York and  elsewhere certainly there was a  breakdown in police effectiveness some  experts have cited a loss and morale  toward our investigations of police  corruption or malfeasance but it's hard  to quantify that theory I think that the  best explanations are largely social the  first line of defense against crime is  not the police force but society as a  whole  parents who restrain their children  successes communities that keep a watch  on the streets the social organization  that once brought some order to New  York's neighborhoods seems to have  weakened dramatically after 1957 the  most famous example of this breakdown  was the 1964 murder of kitty Genovese in  Queens the dying girl's cries for help  were ignored by her neighbors even if  this social hypothesis is correct we  don't know why New York society changed  Jane Jacobs the great urbanist would lay  some blame on public housing projects  which replaced the defensible space of  old tenements with tall towers and open  often empty space in the old  neighborhoods there would be eyes on the  street as people looked out of their  windows from short buildings in the new  towers the streets were divorced from  the apartments and consequently there  are fewer people watching when someone  was mugged my own work with Bruce asset  out documents that street crime is  higher in neighborhoods with taller  buildings even holding income constant  although apartments are actually less  likely to be burglarized than  single-family homes the city responded  to poverty and rising crime by spending  more Lindsay had been elected on a good  government ticket and immediately tried  to rein in spending by getting tough  with public sector unions this Silk  Stocking Republican didn't know the  street brawl that he was going to start  Mike will the tough-as-nails head of New  York's Transit workers went to jail for  leading a 12 day strike in 1966 Lindsay  got an injunction ordering the subways  to start rolling again Lindsay knew how  to work the courts but quill just  ignored the injunction saying that the  judge can drop dead in his black robes I  don't care if I rot in jail I will not  call off the strike  quill won and his workers wages rose by  almost $1 an hour city spending rose  while the city's economy was sputtering  the Lindsay Administration  responded with accounting legerdemain  when Lindsey left office in 1973 the new  mayor a beam former accountant himself  was left to pick up the pieces beam  couldn't do it maybe no one could have  by 1975 New York was on the brink of  bankruptcy beam sought federal help but  President Ford wasn't going to give cash  without some promises that New York  would reform promises that beam couldn't  really make and certainly couldn't keep  when Ford turned him down the daily news  headlines screamed Ford to City drop  dead surely one of the most famous  headlines in history Ford didn't  actually say those words but in those  years it seemed like not just the White  House but history itself was telling New  York City to drop dead New York only  avoided bankruptcy by turning to the  state governor Kerry established the  municipal Assistance corporation which  would keep New York City liquid but  would also take over fiscal control  proud New York had lost the ability to  manage its own finances by the end of  beam's term the city seemed like it was  in total chaos TV cameras during the  1977 World Series caught Bronks  buildings burning the fire department's  ability to respond had been reduced by  budget cuts a summer blackout set off a  crime spree would sharpen contrasted  with a 1966 blackout when the city had  stayed safe  the murderous Son of Sam terrorized the  city in the midst of this Maelstrom Edie  Koch managed to get himself elected  mayor over the discredited beam and the  unforgettable Bella Abzug who once owned  the liberal heart of the city Koch had  also been a liberal lion he became  famous besting the last boss of Tammany  Hall carmine DeSapio but as crime rates  rose Koch morphed into the get tough on  crime candidate 1977 was his year as New  York's voters cast away traditional  ethnic politics and cast away the  liberal hopes of the 1960s the city's  madness needed to be managed and voters  thought that Ed Koch was the man for the  job  you

